From: "Christopher A. Williamson" <contact@chrisaw.io>
Date: Sat, 14 Mar 2026 08:21:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] SurfaceFlinger: Skip mode-change fence check during boot

On devices with multi-rate displays (e.g. 60Hz+120Hz) and HWC
implementations that don't signal present fences before the first
composed frame, the modeset_state_machine code path creates a
deadlock during boot:

1. SF requests 120Hz mode change, setting isModeSetPending=true
2. HWC says refreshRequired=true, so a composite is scheduled
3. In commit(), the isFramePending+isModeSetPending check at line
   2874 returns false, blocking composite() from ever running
4. No frame is ever presented, so the present fence never signals
5. isFramePending stays true, isModeSetPending stays true
6. Result: permanent black screen

This affects devices like the Blackview Shark 8 (MT6789) where HWC's
blankDisplay atomic commit fails with EINVAL and present fences are
never signaled before the first successful composite.

VoltageOS avoids this because it lacks the modeset_state_machine
feature flag (a newer AOSP addition present in GrapheneOS).

Fix by skipping the fence-gated mode-change check during boot
(mBootStage != FINISHED). This is safe because:
- No content is on screen yet, so tearing is impossible
- The mode change will finalize normally once the first frame lands
- After boot finishes, the check resumes protecting against mid-use
  mode change glitches
---
 services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp b/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
index 549d2a4..bb30422 100644
--- a/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
+++ b/services/surfaceflinger/SurfaceFlinger.cpp
@@ -2871,7 +2871,12 @@ bool SurfaceFlinger::commit(PhysicalDisplayId pacesetterId,
     }
 
     // If a mode set is pending and the fence hasn't fired yet, wait for the next commit.
-    if (std::any_of(frameTargets.begin(), frameTargets.end(),
+    // Skip this check during boot to avoid a deadlock on devices with multi-rate displays
+    // (e.g. 60Hz+120Hz) and broken HWC fence signaling: the mode change can't finalize
+    // until a frame is presented, but this check blocks frame presentation until the mode
+    // change finalizes.
+    if (mBootStage == BootStage::FINISHED &&
+            std::any_of(frameTargets.begin(), frameTargets.end(),
                     [this](const auto& pair) FTL_FAKE_GUARD(kMainThreadContext) {
                         const auto [displayId, target] = pair;
                         return target->isFramePending() &&
